tage and a few Republican-sponsored polls showing a much closer race. Race raters call the contest Lean Democratic, and prediction markets give Benson roughly a ninety percent chance of winning.\n\nBenson also holds a clear financial advantage, raising about twice as much as James and entering the fall with roughly five times his cash on hand. James, seeking to become Michigan's first Black governor, has kept the race competitive by emphasizing the economy, which polls show as voters' top concern. The state's even partisan balance means a strong Republican closing argument could narrow the race, but the weight of the evidence favors Benson heading into November.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9339, "margin": 7.39}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9166, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9224, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9193, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8901, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9053, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.911, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9221, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9131, "margin": 6.8}]}